 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.independencia.02.movimientos-independencia",
-  "title": "Se pasivo: los movimientos de independencia",
+  "title": "Preterite and imperfect: telling the war",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "The se-passive treats the wars of independence as a collective phenomenon: uprisings were organized, a conflict was waged, campaigns were carried out, an army was defeated — without crediting a single leader for something that was, in practice, thousands of people's doing. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know the preterite and the imperfect from A2 Unit 22. The preterite tells what happened, step by step (*llamó*, *capturaron*); the imperfect gives the background that was true at the time (*era cura*, *eran pobres*). A war story needs both."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "En México se libró un conflicto casi ininterrumpido entre 1810 y 1821.",
-          "english": "In Mexico, an almost uninterrupted conflict was waged between 1810 and 1821."
+          "spanish": "Hidalgo era cura de Dolores cuando llamó a la población a levantarse.",
+          "english": "Hidalgo was the priest of Dolores when he called the people to rise."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Se organizaron levantamientos contra las autoridades coloniales en distintos momentos y lugares.",
-          "english": "Uprisings against colonial authorities were organized at different times and places."
+          "spanish": "Muchos campesinos eran pobres, y por eso siguieron a Hidalgo.",
+          "english": "Many peasants were poor, and so they followed Hidalgo."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "En Sudamérica se llevaron a cabo campañas militares enormes a lo largo de miles de kilómetros.",
-          "english": "In South America, enormous military campaigns were carried out across thousands of kilometers."
+          "spanish": "Los realistas tenían un ejército mejor, y en 1811 capturaron a Hidalgo.",
+          "english": "The royalists had a better army, and in 1811 they captured Hidalgo."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "En la batalla de Ayacucho se derrotó de manera decisiva al último gran ejército realista del continente.",
-          "english": "At the Battle of Ayacucho, the continent's last great royalist army was decisively defeated."
+          "spanish": "La guerra continuaba cuando Iturbide y Guerrero firmaron el Plan de Iguala.",
+          "english": "The war was still going on when Iturbide and Guerrero signed the Plan of Iguala."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Llevarse a cabo (to be carried out) is a set phrase almost always used in the se-passive or the ordinary passive — you'll rarely see it any other way, which makes it a useful chunk to memorize whole rather than building word by word."
+      "content": "Ask: is it a step in the story (preterite) or the scene around it (imperfect)? *Cuando llamó* is the event; *era cura* is the situation."
     }
   ]
 }
